    "summary": "Smoky China>Rider and soaring second‑set jams dominate the night",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a crystal‑clear China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider and an expansive Truckin' → The Other One → Stella Blue sequence. Jerry’s guitar shines, Phil’s bass bombs, and the band stays tight through lengthy improvisations. Multiple soundboard sources capture the performance with excellent fidelity.",
    "review": "November 20 1973 at the Denver Coliseum finds the Dead in top form, riding a high‑energy wave that carries from the first set straight into a marathon second set. The first half sparkles with tight renditions of classics—Ramble On Rose, Brown Eyed Women and a standout To Lay Me Down—while the Weather Report Suite unfolds with its usual dynamic arc. The real fireworks erupt after the break: China Cat Sunflower segues flawlessly into I Know You Rider, then a thunderous Truckin' launches into an epic The Other One that blossoms into a soaring Mind Left Body jam before resolving in a plaintive Stella Blue. Jerry’s tone is described as crystal‑clear and “perfect,” especially on the extended jams, while Phil’s bass “bombs” through Truckin’ and adds inventive counterpoint in The Other One. Keith’s piano sits unusually forward, giving the music extra texture, and Bob Weir’s rhythm work provides solid grounding throughout. The best‑available soundboard recording captures every nuance with pristine balance, making this a must‑listen for anyone chasing late‑1973 Dead magic.",
